Homework 2 - Mehrdad 

This homework was pretty fun, and I learned a lot, not only about Git but also about this innovative approach to teaching. 
These types of interactive games are really a nice way to make complicated concepts more understandable and easier to learn. 
I’ll definitely try to use a similar approach for teaching in my own field, which has a lot of complicated concepts that are sometimes really hard to explain to students. 
I started the homework by going through the instructions and examples at each step of the Learn Git Branching website. 
For each topic, I first read the explanations of the Git commands and carefully followed the animations to see how commits, branches, and HEAD changed in the diagrams. 
After that, I worked on the exercises where I had to use the commands to reach the target structure as instructed by the game. The Goal to Reach window was also helpful because it showed exactly what I needed to achieve in each exercise.
The first part was about basic Git commands. It started with git commit and showed how each new commit was connected to the previous one. The visualization made it much easier for me to understand the concepts that we went through in class. 
The next level was about branches and how to create and switch between them using commands like git branch and git switch. We had already practiced these commands in class but practicing them interactively helped me better understand how branches work and how to switch between them. 
One example showed that when I made a commit on main, only the main branch moved forward, while the other branch stayed in the same place. After switching to the other branch and making another commit, I could see how the two branches developed separately. 
The next two lessons introduced git merge and git rebase. These were a bit confusing for me in class, but this game made it really easy to understand. I followed the examples using commands such as git merge, git switch and git rebase. It took me some time to understand the difference, but seeing how merge combined the branches and rebase moved the commits to follow another branch was helpful.
In the next section, I learned more about HEAD and how Git refers to previous commits. The tutorial first showed how to detach HEAD using commands like git switch --detach and how to switch back to main. Then it explained relative references, including HEAD^ to move back one commit and HEAD~n to go back n commits. 
I practiced commands like git switch --detach HEAD^ and git branch -f main HEAD~3. It was pretty cool to see how these simple commands allowed me to navigate through commits and move branch pointers. 
This part was a little confusing because I sometimes moved to the wrong commit or used a reference that did not exist. I had to check the diagram and try again. I also learned about git reset and git revert, and how they can be used to undo changes in different ways.
The Remote section introduced how Git works when a repository is shared with other people through platforms like GitHub. This was useful for understanding how to transfer commits between my local repository and a shared remote repository. 
It started by introducing git clone and explaining the difference between local branches and remote branches such as o/main. 
The tutorial then explained git fetch, which gets updates from the remote repository, and git pull, which also integrates those changes into the local branch. I practiced git push to send my local commits to the remote repository. 
There was also an example using git fakeTeamwork to simulate another person making changes. This helped me see what happens when the local and remote repositories have different commits. 
The last few levels were more challenging. In the diverged history exercise, I learned why Git sometimes rejects a push when someone else has already updated the remote repository. I practiced using git pull --rebase followed by git push to solve this. 
In the locked main exercise, I learned that I could create another branch using git checkout -b, push it with git push origin, and move the local main branch back using git branch -f.
Throughout the homework, I checked my progress by comparing the result of each command with the goal diagram. If the branches or HEAD were not in the correct positions, I reviewed the instructions and tried another command. 
Overall, I liked that the website explained the commands first and then let me practice them. The animations were probably the most useful part for me because I could actually see how each command affected the repository and understand why the changes happened. 
I used AI assistance to check the grammar and wording of my process explanation.
